---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadLatest } from "../lib/data";
import { href } from "../lib/format";
const data = loadLatest();
---
<Base title="About the data | Sound Check" description="Where Sound Check's numbers come from, how 'normal' is calculated, and what the alerts mean." regionName={data.region.name} current="about">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About the data</h1>
    <p>Sound Check turns public monitoring data into a plain-language read on the Mississippi Sound: how salty and how warm
      the water is, how hard the rivers are running, how much rain has fallen upstream, and whether any of it is unusual for
      the time of year. It updates every three hours.</p>

    <h2 id="official">Official sources and what Sound Check is</h2>
    <p>Sound Check is a free hobby project. It is not the system of record for anything on this site, and no agency
      reviews or endorses it. Closures, advisories, seasons and marine forecasts are decided and published by the agencies
      below, and where we differ from them, they are right.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Who decides</th><th>How it reaches this page</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Beach advisories and closures</td>
          <td><a href="https://beaches.mdeq.ms.gov/">MDEQ Beach Monitoring Program</a></td>
          <td>Every three hours our pipeline reads the public map service behind MDEQ's site and republishes each beach's status. We do not interpret sample results or issue advisories. A beach showing clear can have had no recent sample.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Oyster reef closures and openings</td>
          <td><a href="https://dmr.ms.gov/shellfish/">MDMR shellfish</a>, oyster information line 228-374-5167</td>
          <td>We have no feed of MDMR's open or closed status. We read the Pearl River stage and forecast from the National Weather Service, estimate rain from Open-Meteo, and compare both with the criteria in MDMR's <a href="https://dmr.ms.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Molluscan-Shellfish-Growing-Waters-Management-Plan_12.2025-edits.pdf">management plan</a>. We never say an area is closed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shrimp seasons</td>
          <td><a href="https://dmr.ms.gov/shrimp/">MDMR shrimp information</a></td>
          <td>We copy the title, date and opening lines of MDMR press releases that mention shrimp seasons. We do not work out which waters are open. The full notice is the rule.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bonnet Carré Spillway openings</td>
          <td><a href="https://www.mvn.usace.army.mil/Missions/Mississippi-River-Flood-Control/Bonnet-Carre-Spillway-Overview/">US Army Corps of Engineers, New Orleans District</a></td>
          <td>No public feed says whether the spillway is open. We compare the Mississippi at New Orleans (National Weather Service stage and forecast) with the level where the Corps has opened it before, and add openings to our list by hand. A river at that level doesn't mean it's open.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wind, waves and marine weather</td>
          <td><a href="https://www.weather.gov/mob/">National Weather Service, Mobile</a></td>
          <td>Our outlooks simplify the NWS point forecast. They are not a marine forecast and not for navigation.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Every figure here is a copy that is at least a few hours old, and the sources revise their own data. If a source can't be
      reached or its data is too old, the section hides instead of showing old numbers. If this site and an agency disagree,
      go with the agency. Do not use Sound Check to decide whether to swim, harvest or head out on the water.</p>

    <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Salinity and water temperature</td><td>USGS stations operated with the Mississippi Department of Marine Resources: Merrill Shell Bank Light, Round Island Light, Point Cadet in Biloxi's Back Bay, and the East Pearl River.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>River flow</td><td>USGS gauges on the Pearl River near Bogalusa and the Pascagoula River at Merrill, plus two smaller coastal rivers: the Wolf River near Landon and the Biloxi River at Wortham. The Jourdan, Tchoutacabouffa and Escatawpa rivers have no live flow gauge; the map draws them, and every river line, from OpenStreetMap.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Air temperature</td><td>National Weather Service observations at Gulfport-Biloxi International Airport. The page checks the weather service for the latest reading each time it loads, so your browser contacts api.weather.gov directly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Beach advisories and closures</td><td>Mississippi Department of Environmental Quality's Beach Monitoring Program, which samples 21 beaches for Enterococcus bacteria and posts advisories for bacteria, debris and algal blooms.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shrimp and oyster season notices</td><td>Mississippi Department of Marine Resources press releases. We show the title, date and opening lines and link to the full notice, which is the rule.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dauphin Island salinity (map, east edge)</td><td>Dauphin Island Sea Lab, served by GCOOS: a daily average of its quality-checked hourly readings. It sits on the Mobile Bay side, about 50 km east of our easternmost station, so it's shown for comparison only and never used to flag our stations.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mississippi River at New Orleans</td><td>National Weather Service stage and two-week forecast at the Carrollton gauge (NORL1). Past spillway openings come from the Corps' operation history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Satellite water clarity and surface temperature</td><td>NOAA CoastWatch: VIIRS water clarity (Kd490) at 750 m and multi-satellite surface temperature at about 2 km. Each map layer is the newest cloud-free view of each spot over the last few days, and the map says which days. Clouds leave gaps.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rain, observed and forecast</td><td>Open-Meteo. Basin totals average a few points across each watershed, so treat them as approximate.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Salinity is calculated from the stations' conductivity readings with the standard seawater formula (PSS-78). That gives a longer and
      more complete history than the separate salinity series, which starts in 2011. Two stations that used to report salinity,
      Gulfport Light and East Ship Island Light, stopped in 2024.</p>

    <h2>What "typical" means</h2>
    <p>For each station and each day of the year, we look at every reading within a week of that date in every year on record
      (back to 1998 at the oldest stations). "Typical" is the middle of those readings. "Unusual" means outside the middle 80%.
      On the charts, the shaded band is that middle 80%.</p>

    <h2>What the alerts mean</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Note</strong>: a reading is unusual for the time of year. Worth knowing, not a problem by itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch, Warning, Alert</strong>: a condition that matters is building. The main one today is freshwater stress on
        oysters: research by the Northern Gulf Institute and the University of Southern Mississippi after the 2019 Bonnet Carré
        Spillway opening found oysters die when salinity stays below about 2 ppt for more than two weeks. We raise a watch at
        5 days, a warning at 10, and an alert at 14.</li>
      <li><strong>Shrimp season lean</strong>: in spring, warmer water has tended to mean an earlier brown shrimp opening.
        Across 2012–2026 that relationship cut the typical miss from about 8 days to about 6, and some years missed by weeks.
        It's a lean, not a forecast. MDMR sets the date from its own trawl sampling.</li>
      <li><strong>Warm-water bacteria (Vibrio)</strong>: Vibrio grows fastest in water at 68°F (20°C) and up with salinity
        between 5 and 25 ppt. When a station has averaged in that range over the last three days, the Coast, Oysters and
        Fishing pages show a short note with health advice from the Mississippi State Department of Health and the CDC.
        That's normal here most days from April through October, so it isn't an alert. We don't measure bacteria, and
        Vibrio can be present outside that range.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Estimates and sensor checks</h2>
    <p>For every salinity station, a model learns what that spot usually reads given the other stations
      and the rivers on the same day. We call it a virtual sensor. It's retrained monthly and scored on the most recent two
      years of data it hasn't seen, and its ranges are adjusted so a "likely range" really holds about 90% of readings.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Estimates</strong> appear only where that held-out error is small: today, Gulfport Light (typically
        within about 2 ppt), whose sensor stopped in 2024, and the Pearl River mouth when its sensor is quiet. Estimates are
        shown in italics with a tilde and a likely range, and hollow markers on the map.</li>
      <li><strong>"Reads saltier than nearby stations suggest"</strong> means a live reading falls outside its likely range.
        It's a note, not an alarm: the model's ranges at some stations are wide.</li>
      <li><strong>Possibly a sensor problem</strong> means a reading is far outside even a 98% range for two of the last
        three days, or saltier than the open Gulf. Those readings never trigger alerts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>East Ship Island, near the Gulf pass, isn't estimated: nothing we measure predicts it well enough.</p>

    <h2>Caveats</h2>
    <p>Readings are provisional until USGS reviews them, and instruments foul or drift. Readings that are physically implausible for
      the Sound, such as water saltier than the open Gulf, are shown with a caveat and never trigger alerts. If a station stops
      reporting, its card says so rather than showing old numbers as current.</p>
    <p>This is a hobby project, not an official source; see <a href="#official">the official sources above</a> for closures,
      advisories, seasons and regulations. Station details are on each <a href={href()}>station page</a>.</p>
  
    <h2>Feedback and source code</h2>
    <p>Sound Check is open source under the MIT license. If a number looks wrong or a sentence is confusing, please
      <a href="https://github.com/ThatOrJohn/ms-sound-check/issues" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Corrections to
      anything that sounds like an official closure or advisory are especially welcome. The code and the reasoning behind
      the design are at <a href="https://github.com/ThatOrJohn/ms-sound-check" rel="noopener">github.com/ThatOrJohn/ms-sound-check</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
